en below 640 px). The iframe is served with `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors` set to the installation's\nallowed origins, so the panel cannot be embedded anywhere else."],[5128,"A short name for the situation, e.g. *Three adults and a dog, 300 euro, rainy forecast*. Required."],[2956,"A short qualifier next to a title — classification, kind, a count."],[2961,"A short qualifier next to a title — classification, kind, a count. Five semantic tones; never a product colour, never clickable."],[5465,"A sign-in completes at core-api's `GET /auth/callback`, which issues a **session** (id, user, tenant, provider,\nissued/expiry, last-seen, client hint) and sets an HttpOnly cookie; `GET /session/context` turns that cookie into\nidentity and tenancy claims — never authorization decisions — and answers **401** with a precise code otherwise\n(`SESSION_REVOKED`, `SESSION_EXPIRED`, `EMAIL_UNVERIFIED` …). **Sessions** is `GET /session/sessions`: the caller's\nown rows plus `current_session_id`. **Revoke other sessions** is `POST /session/sessions:revoke-others`\n(`DELETE /session/sessions/{id}` revokes one); each revoked row is written, audited as `session.revoked` with a\nreason, and published as `nexus.core.session.revoked.v1` so every service refuses that session within its next\nrevocation-feed tick — the *within a minute* on the screen. **Sign out** is `POST /auth/logout`: the row is revoked\nserver-side and the cookie cleared. &#x2A;*Forgot password?** is `POST /auth/password:reset`, which asks the identity\nprovider to send its *update password* mail and always answers **202**; the password and any second factor live\nin the provider, and Core holds no credential for you. The shell never decides you are signed in — it asks\n`/session/context` and follows the answer."],[7416,"a signed pack with a `module` resource; installed in NexCore → Blueprints & Packs"],[724,"A signed statement &#x2A;\"this is the person behind session X, valid until T\"*. NexCore mints it from 