tead — the *Why* panel shows\nthe data arm skipped with *a file is attached; the dataset question needs the Project's governed data*."],[2285,"asr-local · tts-local — speech sidecars"],[1694,"Assign work item"],[4743,"At the end of Build your first Flow a real run stopped at **Review results** — *Waiting for\napproval*. This chapter is about that moment from the other side: you are the person the Flow is waiting for. You\nlearn where a parked run shows up, what the **approval card** tells you before you decide, why an editor sees the\nsame card without buttons, what each decision does to the run, and how the whole story is kept afterwards."],[4694,"At the end of this chapter the Project **Weekend trips** contains a published agent called **Trip planner**. It\nknows what you want from a weekend proposal, it can search the Project's own material, and anyone in the Project can\npick it in the chat composer with `@`. You will have written exactly one sentence — Nexi drafts the rest and you\nreview it."],[4641,"At the end of this chapter the Project **Weekend trips** contains a published skill, **Weekend planning checklist**,\nreachable as `/weekend` in any chat of the Project and attached to the **Trip planner** agent. The agent will\nfollow the checklist every time it writes a proposal — same structure, same completeness, whoever asks."],[3927,"At the end of this chapter you have a Project called **Weekend trips**, one conversation in it with two questions\nanswered, and you know four things every Nexi user needs: where a chat lives, how to choose who answers, how to\nsee *why* an answer says what it says, and how to get back to a chat tomorrow."],[3892,"At the end of this chapter you have a verified Nexi account, you own a new organization, and Nexi has created a\ndefault Project called **General** and a base **Blueprint** for you. You will be able to open NexChat straight away\nand come back to NexCore whenever you want to invite people or change settings."],[1669,"A